WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:11.400
It is December 5, 1757. Frederick the&nbsp;
Great, king of the rising Prussian state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:17.480
rides through the clear, icy winter air. Upon&nbsp;
reaching a small hill near the village of Leuthen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:22.440
he looks out on an overwhelming&nbsp;
sight. As far as the eye can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:28.600
the enemy line stretches across the horizon.&nbsp;
Sixty-six thousand Austrian soldiers—nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:36.080
twice the number he commands—stand ready to face&nbsp;
his exhausted troops. But Frederick has already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:43.080
made his decision. Here, on the frozen fields&nbsp;
of Silesia, he will meet the Austrians, and here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:51.600
he will determine the fate of Prussia. In his&nbsp;
mind, only two outcomes exist: victory or death.&nbsp;

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:06.200
What awaited Frederick was one of the bloodiest&nbsp;
battles of the Age of Enlightenment. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:12.960
this era is often seen as rational, tolerant, and&nbsp;
progressive, warfare was more brutal and deadly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.880
than ever before. Massive armies marched across&nbsp;
Europe and were obliterated in spectacular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:26.120
clashes—like candles held in a blowtorch, to use&nbsp;
an image coined by historian Dennis Showalter.&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.600
This was the age of linear tactics, when soldiers&nbsp;
formed long, thin lines to allow as many muskets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.560
as possible to fire simultaneously. At Leuthen,&nbsp;
the Prussians set an enduring example of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:45.880
these tactics were best executed. The battle is&nbsp;
widely regarded as one of the greatest—perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:51.840
the greatest—military achievements in Europe&nbsp;
during the Ancien Régime. Even Napoleon praised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:59.080
it as “a masterpiece of movements, maneuvers, and&nbsp;
resolution.” This is how modern historiography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:11.880
recounts the story of the Battle of Leuthen.
Chapter 1: The Fertile Fields of Silesia&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.880
The fertile fields of Silesia lay in the eastern&nbsp;
part of the Holy Roman Empire, between Poland and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:24.480
Bohemia. The region was among the most prosperous&nbsp;
in Europe. With its rich agriculture, abundant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.720
coal and ore deposits, and central location&nbsp;
for trade between Eastern and Central Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:37.480
Silesia was a cornerstone of the Prussian economy.
But that productive patch of land had not always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:44.200
belonged to Prussia. Frederick the Great had&nbsp;
only seized the province from Austria in 1740,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:49.920
and Empress Maria Theresa was determined to&nbsp;
reclaim it. When the Seven Years’ War erupted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.160
in 1756, Frederick aimed to force&nbsp;
the Empress into peace negotiations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.160 --> 00:03:00.680
and secure her final renunciation of Silesia&nbsp;
through a decisive victory on the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.040
Though he faced an overwhelming coalition&nbsp;
of France, Russia, Austria, and Saxony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:11.760
he was determined to hold on to his&nbsp;
territory and even expand his kingdom.&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.960
The first year of the war was a disaster for&nbsp;
the Prussians. Despite bitter fighting—most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:23.600
notably in the bloody battles of Lobositz,&nbsp;
Prague, and Kolin—Frederick was unable to repel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:31.200
Austria and its allies. Instead, by the fall&nbsp;
of 1757, a new threat emerged from the west:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:37.400
the Imperial Army and the French were marching&nbsp;
against him. Frederick rode out to meet them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:44.120
encountered the enemy near the village of Roßbach.&nbsp;
In just four hours, in a battle considered one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:50.600
of the most one-sided of the Seven Years’ War, he&nbsp;
crushed an army nearly twice the size of his own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:57.840
Yet while Frederick triumphed in the west,&nbsp;
everything was unraveling in Silesia.&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.480
Frederick’s deputy, August Wilhelm, the&nbsp;
Duke of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel-Bevern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:07.760
was hopelessly outmatched by the Austrians under&nbsp;
Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine and Count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:14.400
Leopold Joseph von Daun. The Austrian forces were&nbsp;
strong enough to not only keep August Wilhelm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:22.000
in check but also simultaneously overrun the key&nbsp;
fortresses. When Breslau, the capital of Silesia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.640
surrendered on November 25, more than half of the&nbsp;
province had fallen back under Austrian control.&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.200
By this point, Prussian morale was utterly&nbsp;
shattered. The soldiers were exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:42.480
hungry, and hopeless. Many saw no reason to keep&nbsp;
fighting and deserted. When August Wilhelm himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.640
was captured during a reconnaissance ride, the&nbsp;
last vestiges of the famous Prussian discipline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:55.200
collapsed. The emaciated troops looted and&nbsp;
pillaged, while their officers largely looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:01.480
the other way. Austria’s advance in Silesia&nbsp;
had pushed Prussia back to square one.&nbsp;

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:07.360
When Frederick learned of this disaster,&nbsp;
he immediately set out to return. General&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:14.960
Hans Joachim von Ziethen rushed ahead to bring the&nbsp;
marauding troops under control. On December 2, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:22.200
king reunited the 13,000 veterans of Roßbach with&nbsp;
the remaining Silesian forces, giving him a total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:30.400
of about 38,000 men. Though still outnumbered and&nbsp;
with troops who could barely march another mile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:35.560
Frederick was determined to strike before the&nbsp;
Austrians could consolidate their position in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:43.080
Silesia. Without the province, the Prussian army&nbsp;
would be deprived of crucial resources. Frederick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:49.080
knew he had to risk a decisive battle despite the&nbsp;
weakness of his army and the approaching winter.&nbsp;

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:58.640
In the Prussian camp, some soldiers huddled around&nbsp;
their campfire, trying to escape the bitter cold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.000
when a stranger joined them. In the flickering&nbsp;
light, they were astonished to recognize him as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:10.440
their king. Frederick sat down, ate with them,&nbsp;
listened to their concerns, laughed at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:17.240
stories, and promised promotions and rewards.
Scenes like this repeated throughout the Prussian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:23.360
camp over the next few days. Frederick,&nbsp;
known as a tough—even relentless—commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:29.360
sought to instill confidence through personal&nbsp;
contact with his soldiers. By speaking kindly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:36.720
rather than punishing them for their behavior,&nbsp;
he gave them hope and a renewed sense of purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:42.320
This psychological strategy, which would later&nbsp;
help earn him the affectionate nickname “Old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.600
Fritz,” was effective: the soldiers took&nbsp;
heart, and morale improved dramatically.&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:57.320
On December 3, Old Fritz gathered his generals&nbsp;
at his headquarters near Parchwitz. Even here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.320 --> 00:07:04.560
his appearance was carefully staged. He presented&nbsp;
himself as an old, weary man in a worn uniform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.080
speaking softly as he delivered&nbsp;
his famous Parchwitz Address:&nbsp;

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.200
“The enemy is in a fortified camp outside&nbsp;
Breslau, which my troops have defended with honor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.040
Tomorrow I will march to attack them. I am&nbsp;
not in a position to account for my actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:25.880
nor for the reasons behind my decision.&nbsp;
I am aware of the difficulties that stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.880 --> 00:07:32.320
in the way of this undertaking, but in my&nbsp;
situation there is only victory or death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:38.840
If we are defeated, all is lost.”
The fate of Prussia would be decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.840 --> 00:07:54.960
in two days—on December 5.
Chapter 2: “Victory or Death”&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.080
Meanwhile, in the Austrian&nbsp;
camp, Charles Alexander and Daun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:02.760
also concluded that a decisive&nbsp;
battle was the best approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:08.360
They aimed to exploit Frederick’s weakness, drive&nbsp;
the Prussians out of Silesia once and for all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:15.080
and bring the war to a definitive end. With a&nbsp;
force far superior to the enemy—66,000 men and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:20.760
210 guns—they chose to leave their strong&nbsp;
defensive position and go on the attack.&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:26.360
Frederick’s decision to take the offensive,&nbsp;
despite the supposedly depleted state of his army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:32.640
took his enemies by surprise. When their scouts&nbsp;
reported that the Prussians were already nearby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.480
the Austrians ordered their troops to march deep&nbsp;
into the night and take up battle formation in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.480 --> 00:08:43.120
open field between the hamlets of Nippern&nbsp;
and Sagschutz. By the time they arrived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:48.680
darkness had fallen, and there was no time&nbsp;
for a proper meal. Hungry and exhausted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:55.200
the soldiers collapsed in their hastily erected&nbsp;
camps—a poor preparation for the battle to come.&nbsp;

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:07.720
As the first light of December 5 broke&nbsp;
across the cold winter landscape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:14.000
the Prussians began their march. Two long columns&nbsp;
of infantry, flanked on both sides by cavalry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.560
advanced in orderly formation toward Leuthen,&nbsp;
while Frederick rode ahead with a vanguard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:26.600
He first encountered the enemy near the village of&nbsp;
Borna. A mixed force of Austrian hussars and Saxon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:33.040
cavalry blocked the advance guard, but a single,&nbsp;
decisive assault scattered them in all directions.&nbsp;

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:39.000
While his men dealt with the prisoners, Frederick&nbsp;
rode ahead to survey the enemy’s position from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:44.760
small hill. The experienced tactician knew&nbsp;
the terrain well—he had trained his troops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:51.160
on these fields in peacetime. Immediately, he&nbsp;
spotted a weakness in the Austrian formation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:58.200
their left wing was exposed. A gap separated&nbsp;
the outermost troops from pond on the far left.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.560
Though the gap had been closed with field&nbsp;
fortifications and reinforced by artillery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.000
it was still a weak spot. In addition,&nbsp;
a line of hills in front of this wing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.840
blocked the Austrians’ line of sight. A&nbsp;
plan began to take shape in the king’s mind.&nbsp;

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:22.480
When Frederick shared his plan with his&nbsp;
generals, a murmur ran through the group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:28.360
From the perspective of a classical&nbsp;
tactician, what he proposed was sheer madness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:33.360
He intended to march his troops along&nbsp;
the Austrian front, break through the gap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.400
between the left wing and the pond, and&nbsp;
then strike at the enemy from the flank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.120
To keep Charles Alexander from immediately&nbsp;
realizing his intentions, Frederick would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.120 --> 00:10:50.640
have to divide his already outnumbered army. The&nbsp;
plan was brilliant, but extraordinarily risky.&nbsp;

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:07.440
Frederick opened the battle with a feint. He sent&nbsp;
his mounted vanguard and several infantry units&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:12.080
to advance against the enemy’s right wing,&nbsp;
creating the illusion of an attack there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:17.800
Charles Alexander and Daun took the bait, shifting&nbsp;
their reserves to the northern end of their line.&nbsp;

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:23.600
Meanwhile, the rest of the Prussian army&nbsp;
moved south, parallel to the enemy front,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:30.440
hidden behind the hills and shrouded in an eerie&nbsp;
silence. This maneuver would have been unthinkable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.440 --> 00:11:37.280
for anyone other than Frederick and his Prussians.&nbsp;
A single alert scout could have spoiled the plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.760
and the risk of becoming entangled a&nbsp;
few hundred meters in front of the enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:46.400
and turning into an easy target was&nbsp;
enormous during such a complex maneuver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:53.840
That the Prussians executed it so flawlessly in&nbsp;
less than two hours is a remarkable testament&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:12:00.120
to their training, their famous drill, and their&nbsp;
discipline. Military historian Christopher Duffy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:07.040
describes the maneuver as “a textbook example&nbsp;
of the Oblique Order,” —a formation where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.960
one wing is held back to concentrate the&nbsp;
army’s striking power on a single point.&nbsp;

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.120
Behind the Austrian line, Charles&nbsp;
Alexander watched the Prussians carefully.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:23.560
In the distance, he noticed a faint&nbsp;
movement—troops seemingly shifting southwest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:29.440
He conferred with Daun, debating what it&nbsp;
might mean and how they should respond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:32.280
For the moment, they decided&nbsp;
to hold their position.&nbsp;

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:39.320
Unlike Frederick, Charles Alexander was unwilling&nbsp;
to risk changing his formation in the face of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:46.880
enemy—and he simply saw no need to do so. He is&nbsp;
said to have remarked mockingly, “Our friends are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:54.600
departing … let them go in peace.” The prince&nbsp;
was so convinced of his army’s superiority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:59.040
that he thoughtlessly dismissed the&nbsp;
movements on the horizon. Frederick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:04.840
he believed, had recognized his inferiority and&nbsp;
was trying to avoid battle at the last minute.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:11.400
The attack in the north, he was sure, was&nbsp;
merely a diversion to cover a Prussian retreat.&nbsp;

00:13:12.680 --> 00:13:17.560
Thanks to Charles Alexander’s hesitation, the&nbsp;
Prussians had all the time they needed to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.560 --> 00:13:23.280
up their positions undisturbed. Shortly after&nbsp;
noon, the foremost units were ready to turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:29.200
and assume battle formation. Frederick had chosen&nbsp;
three infantry battalions for the initial advance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.200 --> 00:13:34.760
two from Infantry Regiment No. 26 and&nbsp;
one from Infantry Regiment No. 13.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:40.280
The choice of these regiments was&nbsp;
deliberate. The 26th, from Pomerania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:46.960
was made up largely of Wends, Slavs living in&nbsp;
northern Germany. As a group with a common origin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:53.120
these men shared a strong sense of community and&nbsp;
loyalty. The 13th, a Berlin regiment known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.120 --> 00:13:57.760
its strict discipline, had earned the telling&nbsp;
nickname: “Thunder and Lightning Regiment.”&nbsp;

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:03.760
The right flank of these men was protected by&nbsp;
three grenadier battalions and one musketeer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:09.240
battalion, while a battery of so-called&nbsp;
“Brummers” —heavy field guns—provided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.240 --> 00:14:15.720
covering fire from nearby Glanzberg. The rest of&nbsp;
the infantry battalions formed a staggered line;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.280 --> 00:14:23.480
each offset 50 paces to the west. Ziethen&nbsp;
secured the rear with 53 cavalry squadrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:28.480
and half a dozen units of light infantry.&nbsp;
By just before 1 p.m., the Prussians had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:33.880
completed their king’s daring maneuver and&nbsp;
were poised to charge the enemy from the flank.&nbsp;

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:40.480
Frederick intended to strike quickly,&nbsp;
unexpectedly, and decisively. When he ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.480 --> 00:14:46.760
the assault troops to advance, he is said to have&nbsp;
told the standard bearers: "Boys, do you see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:53.600
whitecoats over there? All you’ve got to do is go&nbsp;
for them with the bayonet and run them out. I’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:15:08.080
support you with the whole army. It’s win or die!"
Chapter 3: The Bloody Fields of Leuthen&nbsp;

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:12.520
The Austrian left wing was commanded&nbsp;
by General Franz Leopold von Nádasdy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:17.000
When the experienced Hungarian noticed&nbsp;
the Prussian movement toward his position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:21.880
he immediately sent a messenger to&nbsp;
Charles Alexander requesting support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:27.520
His commander, however, refused—even&nbsp;
after Nádasdy followed up several times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:33.760
stressing the urgency of the situation. The&nbsp;
general was left to face the attack alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:40.040
He could only oppose the Prussian assault with the&nbsp;
mixed troops from the Holy Roman Empire stationed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.040 --> 00:15:46.280
on his left wing—mainly Württembergers and&nbsp;
Bavarians. Nádasdy had deliberately placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:51.160
them at the far end of his section, because&nbsp;
he considered them unreliable and hoped to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.160 --> 00:15:58.360
keep them as far from the action as possible.&nbsp;
Now, they were in the very eye of the storm.&nbsp;

00:16:04.280 --> 00:16:08.840
Nádasdy and his officers did everything they&nbsp;
could to save the situation without the prince’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:15.200
support. They swung their wing 90 degrees to&nbsp;
the left and took cover behind a shallow trench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:21.960
securing at least a defensible position. Still,&nbsp;
the outlook was grim. Frederick’s spearhead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:28.080
was advancing straight for their weakest point.
The men on the new front line met the Prussians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:32.480
with a heavy volley of fire, holding&nbsp;
their ground bravely for several minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:38.320
But when the first ranks of Prussian soldiers&nbsp;
emerged from the thick smoke of gunpowder like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.320 --> 00:16:45.080
machines with fixed bayonets, the resistance&nbsp;
collapsed almost instantly. The Württembergers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.080 --> 00:16:50.560
on the front line panicked and fled, and the&nbsp;
Bavarians behind them poured into the retreat.&nbsp;

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:57.240
The Prussian army advanced rapidly—but without&nbsp;
haste—under the cover of its mobile artillery.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:03.520
Soon, they made contact with the Austrians,&nbsp;
beginning with the 26th Infantry Regiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:09.000
These men were commanded by Prince Moritz von&nbsp;
Anhalt-Dessau. When they were almost out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:15.240
ammunition—the 60 rounds each soldier carried were&nbsp;
quickly spent in such a fast-paced engagement—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:20.800
prince ordered his regiment to fall&nbsp;
back. Allegedly, the soldiers refused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:26.840
and sent an officer to fetch fresh cartridges.&nbsp;
The man returned in record time, and the 26th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:26.840 --> 00:17:32.720
pressed forward with full ammunition pouches.
Whether or not this episode happened exactly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:39.280
told, it illustrates some of Frederick’s crucial&nbsp;
innovations. In the battles of Prague and Kolin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:44.280
his troops had suffered heavy losses while&nbsp;
approaching the enemy lines. At Leuthen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:50.560
he had them advance under constant fire, allowing&nbsp;
them to weaken the Austrians before first contact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.560 --> 00:17:56.480
and forcing the enemy to remain under cover.&nbsp;
Such a tactic had rarely been attempted before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:56.480 --> 00:18:03.000
as it required extraordinary discipline: soldiers&nbsp;
had to fire while maintaining a steady advance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:08.800
rather than seeking shelter to engage the enemy&nbsp;
from relative safety. To meet the increased need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:13.600
for ammunition, Frederick brought supply wagons&nbsp;
that followed the advancing infantry alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:19.840
the artillery. This is what enabled the officer&nbsp;
of the 26th to resupply his comrades so quickly.&nbsp;

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:25.160
As the infantry began to roll up the&nbsp;
Austrian formation from south to north,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:29.920
the artillery demonstrated its remarkable&nbsp;
mobility. This was especially true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:35.280
of Frederick’s horse artillery, which was&nbsp;
known as “flying artillery” for its speed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.280 --> 00:18:40.560
The artillerymen constantly repositioned their&nbsp;
guns, keeping pace with the infantry and providing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:45.760
continuous cover for the advancing troops.
When Nádasdy saw that his front line was beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:50.760
to give way, he attempted to salvage the situation&nbsp;
by throwing his cavalry against the Prussian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:56.280
flank. The horsemen gathered and galloped around a&nbsp;
small wood at the eastern edge of the battlefield.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:02.200
After a few minutes, they encountered Ziethen’s&nbsp;
cavalry, strategically positioned behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.200 --> 00:19:07.080
right wing for this very scenario.&nbsp;
The foothills and uneven terrain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:13.560
made a coordinated attack impossible, and chaos&nbsp;
ensued. Only when Ziethen committed the last of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:20.120
his 53 squadrons did his men gain the upper hand.
As the Austrians finally broke off their assault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:25.640
the Prussians pursued them to their own lines and&nbsp;
charged the infantry, which was retreating slowly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:32.280
They quickly captured around 2,000 demoralized&nbsp;
Austrians. This shattered the last resistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:39.560
forcing Nádasdy to fall back along the whole line.
Only when his left flank began to disintegrate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.560 --> 00:19:44.760
did Charles Alexander realize what was&nbsp;
really going on. He attempted to reinforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:49.320
the weakened wing by sending several&nbsp;
infantry regiments from the second line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.320 --> 00:19:55.720
but when this proved futile, he was forced to&nbsp;
resort to more drastic measures. Finally, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.720 --> 00:20:02.720
had no choice but to order the entire formation to&nbsp;
turn 90 degrees and form a new front facing south.&nbsp;

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:08.960
Much to his regret, it quickly became clear that&nbsp;
the Austrians were less adept at maneuvering under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.960 --> 00:20:15.520
pressure than their opponents. Instead of moving&nbsp;
in an orderly fashion, the battalions hurried in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:21.400
straight lines to their new positions, arriving&nbsp;
exhausted. Lacking clear orders, they remained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:28.240
standing where they arrived rather than forming&nbsp;
proper lines. This made them easy targets for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.240 --> 00:20:34.040
Prussian artillery on the nearby hills. The&nbsp;
guns on Butterberg, in particular, inflicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:39.800
heavy losses during these crucial minutes. By the&nbsp;
time the maneuver was complete, the Austrians had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:45.880
inadvertently stretched their front line to nearly&nbsp;
10 kilometers, leaving their forces overextended.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:53.480
Now, finally, the two armies stood face to face.
What they saw is exactly what you'll find in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.480 --> 00:20:58.760
new colouring book—detailed illustrations of&nbsp;
a Prussian Grenadier, Frederick the Great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:04.480
and a light infantry Jäger. We made it for people&nbsp;
who want to do more with our artwork than just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:10.440
watch videos. Pick up a pen or pencil and bring&nbsp;
these soldiers and commanders to life yourself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:15.560
There's also artwork from other eras in there,&nbsp;
including pieces from videos we haven't released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:21.960
yet—so it's a bit of a sneak peek too. The paper&nbsp;
quality is solid, the illustrations are intricate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.960 --> 00:21:26.760
and it's genuinely great for studying uniforms,&nbsp;
equipment, and the commanders who shaped these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:32.600
battles. A nice way to step away from a screen&nbsp;
for a while—and if you've been following along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:36.360
you might recognize a few of&nbsp;
those Prussian soldiers already.&nbsp;

00:21:40.080 --> 00:21:45.160
At 3:30 p.m., the Prussians launched&nbsp;
their attack on the new Austrian line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:49.200
This marked the beginning of the second&nbsp;
phase of the battle. Encouraged by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:54.840
characteristic droning of their field guns,&nbsp;
Frederick’s infantry advanced inexorably.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:55.400 --> 00:22:01.960
Their momentum carried them up to Leuthen in less&nbsp;
than an hour, where their progress finally slowed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:08.000
The village, though small and only lightly&nbsp;
fortified, proved a formidable obstacle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:12.160
The Austrians had entrenched themselves&nbsp;
thoroughly and held a clear advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:17.240
behind the cover of the buildings. Frederick’s&nbsp;
men opened fire from a distance, while a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.240 --> 00:22:22.800
assault force moved forward to break through&nbsp;
the barricaded gates. Once the entry was forced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:27.160
the rest of the troops advanced and formed&nbsp;
lines on the narrow streets of the village.&nbsp;

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:34.320
What followed was an infantryman’s nightmare.&nbsp;
Muzzle flashes lit up every corner, roof,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:40.240
and window. The soldiers fought their way through&nbsp;
the streets, step by step, storming one building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:45.800
after another, while the enemy lurked behind every&nbsp;
wall and on every landing, ready to strike with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:51.560
bayonets or rifle butts. When they reached&nbsp;
the church, the bloodshed reached its peak.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.560 --> 00:22:55.920
An Austrian battalion had turned the building&nbsp;
into a fortress, entrenching itself behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:01.440
the cemetery wall. There it held out against&nbsp;
the Prussians until their artillery advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:08.600
and reduced the wall to rubble. The Austrians lost&nbsp;
ground, but both sides paid dearly for every step.&nbsp;

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:15.600
As his men withdrew from Leuthen, Charles&nbsp;
Alexander made one final attempt to turn the tide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.680
He had the infantry form a&nbsp;
new line behind the village&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:23.680
and sent the cavalry against the Prussian&nbsp;
left flank. This spot was vulnerable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:29.560
since the infantry there had turned toward&nbsp;
Leuthen. As 70 squadrons of cavalry galloped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:33.880
toward the Prussian line, the air shook&nbsp;
with the thunder of hundreds of hooves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:40.400
For a moment, it seemed as if the horsemen would&nbsp;
sweep into Frederick’s exposed flank unchecked.&nbsp;

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:47.880
Then, suddenly, the cavalry of the Prussian left&nbsp;
wing appeared between Schleierberg and Butterberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:53.960
striking the Austrians from the side.&nbsp;
The Bayreuth Dragoons made contact first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:59.720
Their impact forced the Austrians to slow&nbsp;
down, and a fierce fight erupted. The dragoons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:04.760
had the element of surprise, but they were&nbsp;
outnumbered and quickly came under heavy pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.760 --> 00:24:11.120
as the Austrians regained their composure. At&nbsp;
that moment, Friedrich Wilhelm von Driesen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:17.040
commander of the Prussian left wing, committed&nbsp;
his last cuirassiers to join the attack. Together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:23.320
the 40 Prussian squadrons drove the Austrians&nbsp;
north—right into the ranks of their own infantry.&nbsp;

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:30.640
This was too much for Charles Alexander’s&nbsp;
already faltering troops. The cold hand of panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:37.240
seized their hearts. One battalion after another&nbsp;
grew anxious, laid down their arms, and fled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:37.760 --> 00:24:43.320
The few units that remained steadfast were&nbsp;
overrun by Frederick’s advancing infantry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:46.560
while those in retreat were&nbsp;
cut down by the cavalry.&nbsp;

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:53.880
As evening approached, heavy snowfall began. A&nbsp;
white blanket slowly covered the scene of battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:59.760
hiding the bloody fields of Leuthen.&nbsp;
Then, breaking the oppressive silence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.760 --> 00:25:04.640
one of the men began to sing “Now Thank We All&nbsp;
Our God” (“Nun danket alle Gott”). According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:11.080
to legend, the soldiers joined in, one&nbsp;
after another, until the hymn—forever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:17.560
remembered as the “Choral of Leuthen”—resounded&nbsp;
across the battlefield from thousands of throats.&nbsp;

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:24.720
In a single day, Frederick the Great had regained&nbsp;
everything his enemies had taken from him over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:24.720 --> 00:25:30.800
the course of a year. Charles Alexander could&nbsp;
hardly believe it. He withdrew from Silesia with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:36.800
the remainder of his troops. On December 20, the&nbsp;
garrison of Breslau surrendered, following him.&nbsp;

00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:43.400
Leuthen was one of the bloodiest battles&nbsp;
of the 18th century. Within just hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.400 --> 00:25:50.640
more than 4,000 men perished and 12,000 were&nbsp;
wounded. Yet above all, it was a masterpiece.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:57.200
The Prussian army had flawlessly executed its&nbsp;
king’s daring plan, demonstrating exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:03.560
discipline and skill. The battle became a textbook&nbsp;
example of linear tactics and the oblique order.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:04.120 --> 00:26:09.400
Decisive action, intimate knowledge of the&nbsp;
terrain, and the extraordinary mobility of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:09.400 --> 00:26:14.320
the artillery combined to produce one of&nbsp;
the century’s most impressive victories.&nbsp;

00:26:14.320 --> 00:26:17.440
Although the Battle of Leuthen&nbsp;
did not compel Maria Theresa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:17.440 --> 00:26:21.800
to back down and thus failed to achieve&nbsp;
Frederick’s immediate diplomatic goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.800 --> 00:26:27.320
it had a profound impact on the political&nbsp;
order of Europe—and indeed, the whole world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:28.200 --> 00:26:33.440
On the frozen fields of Leuthen, Prussia&nbsp;
demonstrated that it could stand toe-to-toe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.440 --> 00:26:39.120
with the continent’s leading nations, laying&nbsp;
the foundation for its rise to a great power.

